Testing that regex extension macros are substituted only outside of
bracket set expressions, where a backslash there merely quotes the
following character.

-- Expect stdout --
[
	"/ \\d \\D [\\d \\D] /",
	"/ \\s \\S [\\s \\S] /",
	"/ \\w \\W [\\w \\W] /",
	[
		"5"
	],
	[
		"d"
	],
	null,
	[
		" "
	],
	[
		"s"
	],
	[
		"a"
	],
	[
		"w"
	]
]
-- End --

-- Testcase --
{%
	printf("%.J\n", [
		/ \d \D [\d \D] /,
		/ \s \S [\s \S] /,
		/ \w \W [\w \W] /,
		match("5", /\d/),
		match("d", /[\d]/),
		match("5", /[\d]/),
		match(" ", /\s/),
		match("s", /[\s]/),
		match("a", /\w/),
		match("w", /[\w]/)
	]);
%}
-- End --


Testing that only the byte right behind the opening bracket of a character
list negates it, and that a right bracket in leading position - and in the
position behind a negation - is an ordinary member. A second caret therefore
denotes itself, which makes `[^^]` the list of everything but a caret.

-- Expect stdout --
[
	"/[^^]/",
	[
		"a"
	],
	null,
	"/[]]/",
	[
		"]"
	],
	"/[^]]/",
	null,
	[
		"a"
	],
	"/[a^]/",
	[
		"^"
	]
]
-- End --

-- Testcase --
{%
	printf("%.J\n", [
		/[^^]/,
		match("a", /[^^]/),
		match("^", /[^^]/),
		/[]]/,
		match("]", /[]]/),
		/[^]]/,
		match("]", /[^]]/),
		match("a", /[^]]/),
		/[a^]/,
		match("^", /[a^]/)
	]);
%}
-- End --


